<p>Lebanese officials, media colleagues, and several international news outlets said journalist Amal Khalil was killed in southern Lebanon after an Israeli strike hit a house in al-Tiri where she had taken shelter; another journalist, photojournalist Zeinab Faraj, was injured. Lebanon&#x27;s prime minister and information minister called the killing a war crime and said strikes also impeded or targeted rescuers trying to reach her, while the Israeli military said it was reviewing the incident. The death came during a fragile truce in the Israel-Hezbollah conflict. Al-Monitor reported that the strike happened despite a 10-day ceasefire and just hours before a second meeting between Lebanese and Israeli representatives with US envoys in Washington, where Beirut was expected to seek an extension. The case has drawn broad condemnation and renewed scrutiny of protections for journalists and first responders in conflict zones, even as cross-border diplomatic contacts suggest channels for de-escalation remain open.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/iV9Cp4IEqXcHQqm9PpbdlUj8dKu4NWpku82xfVgkExDm_SoYcFehKrepkstNU7QvJBnBDBnZvhXvbhjFZoYc_VvD-5qJSqwFfdzYHsDC8_cpELkQ1h07D97xh4qe94Kqbd9KaSy-nMaCbJDoHUhN5K6pu3ZCOZRZggJ0jP1rxL1tvWGAE8CfaXXmJrJE1HcaWlbZuO0ZonHCIkEZjLoNJJ-VMYuhDmU2kFOlsGpigo61Cw' alt='Amal Khalil, who worked for Al-Akhbar, reporting near a destroyed bridge last month. She was killed on Wednesday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Rescue delays: A Lebanese Red Cross official told AFP that teams rescued Zeinab Faraj but withdrew before reaching Khalil because of a warning strike. Rescuers later sought access through the ceasefire monitoring mechanism and UNIFIL before recovering her body.</li><li>Pattern alleged: Prime Minister Nawaf Salam said attacks on media workers in southern Lebanon reflected an established pattern, not isolated incidents, a claim echoed by Lebanese officials and rejected by Israel in past allegations.</li><li>Diplomatic timing: The strike came ahead of a second Washington meeting involving Lebanese and Israeli ambassadors and US officials, with Beirut expected to press for an extension of the ceasefire arrangement.</li></ul><blockquote>The targeting of journalists is a grave crime and a blatant violation of international humanitarian law.</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Lebanese government: Prime Minister Nawaf Salam described the killing as a war crime and said strikes on Red Cross vehicles kept rescuers from reaching the scene quickly. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yvn036evlo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>Israeli military: The Israeli army said it was reviewing the incident after Lebanese leaders accused it of deliberately targeting a journalist and rescuers. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/lebanon-leaders-accuse-israel-war-crime-after-journalist-killed'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Committee to Protect Journalists: The group called Khalil&#x27;s killing a grave breach of international humanitarian law and framed the case as part of the wider dangers reporters face in conflict zones. (<a href='https://novaramedia.com/2026/04/23/israel-kills-top-journalist-then-attacks-rescuers-trying-to-save-her/'>Novara Media</a>)</li><li>Zeinab Faraj: The injured photojournalist publicly recounted Khalil&#x27;s final moments, offering a firsthand account of the strike&#x27;s immediate aftermath. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/23/journalist-recalls-israeli-strike-killing-amal-khalil-in-southern-lebanon'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://novaramedia.com/2026/04/23/israel-kills-top-journalist-then-attacks-rescuers-trying-to-save-her/'>Israel Kills Top Journalist Then Attacks Rescuers Trying to Save Her</a> - novaramedia.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/lebanon-israel-war-crime-journalist-killed-drone-strike/'>Lebanon accuses Israel of war crime in killing of journalist</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='